{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>A hundred years and twenty years</b> — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 23:1): for this reason the word שנה (\"year\") is written for each and every total, to tell you that each one is expounded on its own: at a hundred she was like a twenty-year-old with regard to sin, and at twenty she was like a seven-year-old with regard to beauty. And so he wrote concerning the years of Abraham's life (Rashi on Genesis 25:7). But this midrash of his is not correct, for behold, in the years of Ishmael's life (ibid. v. 17) it is said identically to the years of Abraham's life, yet they were not equal in goodness — rather, he was initially wicked and did repentance at the end. Furthermore, the repetition of שנה שנה (\"year, year\") serves to distinguish the meanings, and it is not expounded to equate them. Rather, \"שנה שנה ושנים\" is simply the manner of the language. And what they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 58:1), \"at a hundred like a twenty-year-old with regard to sin\" — they expounded this not from this but from the superfluity of the expression where he repeated and said \"the years of Sarah's life,\" for he generalized them and equated them. And they would not expound thus concerning Abraham."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "In the phrase 'שנה שנה ושנים,' Ramban is quoting the repeated pattern of the word שנה in the verse as a general linguistic phenomenon; the phrase is rendered literally without expanding on which specific verses he has in mind."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:33:23.496726237Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}